WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.860
DOMINIC PREUSS: Good morning,
everyone, or good afternoon.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.770
It's been a long day already.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.220
My name is Dominic
Preuss I'm the product

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.890
lead for storage and databases
on the Google Cloud platform.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.950
Very excited to speak
to you guys today

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:30.590
about how to you scale
your ideas from concepts

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:31.950
to petabyte.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.300
The goal of this talk
is really to give you

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.330
an overview of our storage
offerings and our databases,

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.234
and to give you a set of
reference architectures

00:00:40.234 --> 00:00:41.900
that you can use when
you're considering

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.346
how to build your applications.

00:00:43.346 --> 00:00:44.470
These are not prescriptive.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.370
These are just some
things to give you

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.860
an idea of how people are
structuring these things so

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.526
that when you are
getting ready to bring

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:52.020
your applications
to Google Cloud,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.250
you've got a starting point.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.850
I want to start by talking
about the Google data platform.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.380
So when we talk about
the data platform,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.820
we really think of it
in a very holistic way.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.060
We don't think about individual
products or silos of data.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.870
The idea here is that we have an
integrated, no ops, cloud data

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.100
platform that allows you
to build your applications.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.150
And most importantly, it's
about you bringing your data

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.250
to the Google Cloud
platform in the way that

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.830
makes sense for you.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.740
And then us making
sure that we offer

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.170
you tools that allow you to
move, transform, and derive

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.940
value from your data, regardless
of where it is the platform.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.730
A very good example
of this is making

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.011
sure-- we've talked a lot
about machine learning--

00:01:38.011 --> 00:01:40.010
where are you going to
store your training data?

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.370
And all of that information
has to be in the platform

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:43.120
somewhere.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.830
And so how do you
go about doing that?

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.770
So as a level set, let's talk
about the portfolio of storage

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.669
offerings and databases.

00:01:52.669 --> 00:01:54.710
First of all, the one that
most people know about

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.360
is Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.177
So this is our object
storage system.

00:01:59.177 --> 00:02:01.260
It's designed for both
structured and unstructured

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.480
data.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.550
And people use it, obviously,
for serving static content

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.860
like images and
video, but it's also

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.850
used for archival, backups,
and as an intermediate data

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:18.520
location for data
processing jobs.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.780
Once we get into the
databases, our oldest offering,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.105
original offering, or one
of our earliest offerings,

00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:25.860
is Cloud SQL.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.181
So if you need a managed MySQL
solution-- again, no ops,

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:30.680
you spin up an
instance and you have

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.920
a database-- Cloud SQL
is the solution for you.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.560
This is really useful
if you're using

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.580
a web framework or some
existing application that

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.905
requires MySQL, or some
other off the shelf RDBMS.

00:02:42.905 --> 00:02:47.890
And by RDBMS, I mean Relational
Database Management System.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.000
If you want a NoSQL solution
and want to go down that path,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.970
we offer two solutions.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.730
One is Cloud Datastore.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.460
Cloud Datastore really
serves the document store use

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.120
case in our platform.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.300
So it's great for mobile
applications, web applications.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.370
If you have a lot
of JSON data, this

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.960
is a great place where you can
basically start from scratch

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.540
and scale up your application.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.430
The other NoSQL application
that is our newest

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:16.620
is Cloud Bigtable.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.900
It's currently in beta.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.950
This is a wide-column store
that provides an HBase client

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.880
compatibility for you to build
your analytics applications.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.980
This is really great for time
series data, metrics data,

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.610
things like advertising
technology, financial services,

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.045
and internet of things.

00:03:36.045 --> 00:03:38.140
If you get a chance
tomorrow to attend

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.166
the talk by FIS about Bigtable,
I highly encourage it.

00:03:42.166 --> 00:03:43.790
Really compelling
use case that they've

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.800
been able to use it for.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.190
And the last one is cache.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.230
So if you need an
in-memory key value store,

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.337
App Engine Memcache
is our solution

00:03:51.337 --> 00:03:53.170
to allow you to put a
caching layer in front

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.710
of your application.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.885
Commonly, this is
used in web, mobile,

00:03:56.885 --> 00:03:57.885
and gaming applications.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:01.500
Great.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.120
So how do I get started?

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.290
So if you have an
idea and you want

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.940
to prototype it or build
something that you can share,

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.177
how would I do that?

00:04:09.177 --> 00:04:11.510
So the simplest architecture
that you could put together

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.000
is a set of clients.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.660
And this just might be
yourself or one or two friends.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:20.980
And if you go down-- generally,
we see our developers split

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.730
into two different use cases.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:25.160
Either they feel very
familiar with MySQL

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.100
and relational
databases, or they want

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.430
to develop NoSQL solutions.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.190
So I'm going to talk
about both use cases

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:33.430
so that we can talk about
those as architectures.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.100
We'll start with NoSQL.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.950
So the simplest thing you can
do is spin up an App Engine

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:41.100
application and store
your application data

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.540
in Datastore and your static
data in Cloud Storage.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.010
And so this should allow you,
with almost no configuration,

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.760
to get up and
running, storing data

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.840
that you can share
it back to a web

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.540
application or a
mobile application

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.610
just as a prototype.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.745
Now, if you wanted to go down
the relational database path,

00:05:00.745 --> 00:05:02.120
the way that you'd
do this is you

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.090
would spin up Compute Engine
to use whatever framework

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:05.590
you want.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:07.330
Now, this could be
Node And we just

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:08.690
introduced Node in App Engine.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:12.060
This could be Python Django
if you're a Python developer.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.030
It could be WordPress
that you're running

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.150
on a Compute Engine instance.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.370
And then you would,
again, set up a cloud SQL.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.250
So you actually have
to think a little bit.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.200
You have to set up an
instance, pick a size,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.020
and tell us how much
storage you want.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.520
And then you would
use Cloud Storage

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.311
just like you would
with the other solution

00:05:28.311 --> 00:05:29.950
to store your static content.

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:33.550
So this can literally go from
zero to serving applications

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:34.905
in an hour.

00:05:34.905 --> 00:05:36.280
This is the simplest
way that you

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:41.170
can get up and running on Google
Cloud and start serving data.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.440
Now, if you take that
concept and you're like,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.640
oh, this is interesting.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.181
And I want to scale it out and
start to move it to production

00:05:47.181 --> 00:05:49.580
and share it with more than
just me and a couple friends,

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.350
you have to start getting more
complicated architectures.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.200
If you're going
down the MySQL path,

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.660
good news is App Engine
and Datastore will just

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:58.482
scale for you.

00:05:58.482 --> 00:05:59.940
You don't have to
make any changes.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.220
You just send it more
traffic, store more data,

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.902
and it'll scale for you.

00:06:03.902 --> 00:06:05.110
Same thing for Cloud Storage.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.180
These are fully
managed, leave it

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.820
and forget it type applications
that allow you to scale up.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:13.640
So your architecture is very
similar for the NoSQL path.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.020
If you're going down the
relational database path

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.909
and you need to
scale, and let's just

00:06:17.909 --> 00:06:19.450
assume you have more
traffic than you

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.034
can put through a single VM, you
would put a Google Cloud load

00:06:22.034 --> 00:06:24.449
balancer-- there's a couple
talks that would have happened

00:06:24.449 --> 00:06:26.470
today about this-- in
front of several VMs

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:31.020
running WordPress, Django,
Node, Rails, whatever framework

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:32.140
you happen to be running.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.200
And then speaking
back to Cloud SQL.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.580
Now with Cloud SQL,
when you set it up,

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:39.090
if you are running in a
production environment,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.670
you add a failover instance.

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.380
And that failover instance
gives you high availability.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.250
So again, in a
fully managed way,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:49.000
we'll give you a
multi-instance MySQL Cluster,

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.949
a set of replicas
that handle failover,

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:53.990
and if, for some reason
there's an issue with one

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.290
of with your primary
Cloud SQL instance,

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.640
we will failover to
the failover replica

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.350
so that you'll
have no down time.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.290
This is all done with
a couple of clicks

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:05.410
when you set up
your MySQL instance.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.150
Again, Cloud Storage for
all your static content.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:09.887
Really the bedrock
of all of these

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:11.470
architectures for
your static content.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.490
Great.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:18.360
So now I've gotten up
to a gigabyte of data.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.010
I've got a bunch of
data in my system.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.000
And what am I get to do now?

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.180
So if you are continuing
to go down the RDBMS path,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.630
if you've got MySQL
running, you now

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:32.660
have lots of websites
and lots of apps

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:35.340
all talking to your application.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.820
So one thing, again, Cloud
Load Balancer-- and you

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.350
could do this with HAProxy or
whatever your favorite load

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.581
balancer is, but if you're
in the managed world where

00:07:42.581 --> 00:07:44.080
you want Google to
manage it for you

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.620
and take advantage of our
amazing Site Reliability

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.490
Engineering
organization, then you

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.270
would use the
Cloud Load Balancer

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.580
to put in front of your VMs.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.260
Again, to shard
your request traffic

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:56.590
to the various compute pieces.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.580
And then go down to
the Cloud SQL level.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.880
Now, Cloud SQL, it starts
to get more interesting.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.860
Once you get above
the amount of traffic

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.642
that you can put
through a single VM,

00:08:05.642 --> 00:08:07.350
then you have to start
thinking about how

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:08.700
to shard your traffic.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.095
And this is where
MySQL starts-- you

00:08:11.095 --> 00:08:13.470
have to take advantage of some
of the more advanced MySQL

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:14.127
features.

00:08:14.127 --> 00:08:15.710
So in this case,
what you'd want to do

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:18.030
is have your Cloud SQL instance.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.340
And you'd want to add
some read replicas.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:22.100
So Cloud SQL offers
you the ability

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.860
to add as many read
replicas as you

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:26.040
want for your master instance.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.610
And then at the
application layer,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:31.150
you would want to shard your
write traffic and your read

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:31.800
traffic.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.950
So all of your write traffic
would go to your master node.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.220
So you could scale up to
the max number of requests,

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.970
max number of write
operations that you

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:44.110
can put through a single GC EVM,
which is what Cloud SQL Second

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:46.280
Generation uses under the hood.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:49.140
And then add read replicas to
meet the amount of read traffic

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:49.960
that you have.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.100
So this basically gives
you a certain level

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:56.230
of horizontal scalability to
split your write workloads

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:58.970
and your read workloads.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:00.820
Again, all of this is
fully managed for you.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:03.210
If something happens
to your read replica,

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.500
Google Site Reliability
Engineers will fix it.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.400
If something happens to
your master instance,

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.690
then we will take your
failover instance,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.240
set it up to be read-write,
and then move the IP address

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.331
over so that you'll have the
minimum amount of downtime.

00:09:17.331 --> 00:09:18.580
You don't have to do anything.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.030
This all happens for
you automatically.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:21.530
It's all part of
the managed service

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:25.580
that Google offers for
Google Cloud Storage.

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:29.200
Some interesting things about
Cloud SQL Second Generation.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.240
We released it in
December of last year.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:33.520
It's currently in beta.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:36.540
It is our fully
managed MySQL service.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.689
It's 3 and 1/2 [INAUDIBLE]
of availability.

00:09:38.689 --> 00:09:40.730
And one of the things we
just released that we're

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.202
very excited about is Hot Grow.

00:09:43.202 --> 00:09:45.660
So what's great about this is
in the situation where you've

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:47.430
gone from a gigabyte
to a terabyte,

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.280
let's say that
you only specified

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:52.310
two gigabytes of storage
for your MySQL instance.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:54.210
Now, if you were rolling
your own instance

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:57.190
and wanted to increase
the amount of disk,

00:09:57.190 --> 00:09:59.470
you'd have to create
a new instance,

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:01.930
or shut down your
instance, create a snapshot

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:05.400
of your persistent disk volume.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.964
Bring up a new PD volume
with that snapshot.

00:10:07.964 --> 00:10:10.380
Re-attach it to the VM, and
then you could continue going.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.171
But that, obviously,
requires a lot of work

00:10:12.171 --> 00:10:14.090
and actually create
some downtime.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:17.370
With How Grow on Cloud
SQL, you can literally

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:21.540
go from 10 gigabytes to 100
gigabytes to a terabyte,

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.630
all the way up to 10 terabytes,
with no downtime on the VM.

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.130
You can dynamically increase
the amount of capacity

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:31.280
that you need for Cloud SQL
and for you MySQL instance

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.030
without having any downtime.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:34.870
So we're doing the
best that we can

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.860
to keep you from having to
make any decisions upfront,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.180
and allowing you the flexibility
to increase your capacity

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:42.740
to meet whatever
load that you have.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:49.330
So now, NoSQL.

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:51.050
So if you're going
down the NoSQL path,

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.880
again, the architecture
looks remarkably similar.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.630
You've got a number of websites,
mobile applications speaking

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:57.820
to your App Engine app.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:00.810
And then you're storing all
of your data in Datastore.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.040
Now, some things you should
think about in Datastore.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.372
Once you're starting to get
above a gigabyte of data,

00:11:05.372 --> 00:11:07.580
and you're starting to get
to more meaningful amounts

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:09.232
of data, Datastore
offers you a number

00:11:09.232 --> 00:11:11.065
of features that helps
with your performance

00:11:11.065 --> 00:11:12.200
and allows you to tune.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:15.140
So things like setting
up composite indexes,

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:16.830
and doing other
maintenance things

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.740
that you can do to
look at, do I need

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:20.850
indexes, what does
my traffic look like,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.540
what is the performance
of my traffic,

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.340
what kind of
characteristics am I seeing?

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:25.840
And starting to use
some of the more

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:29.730
advanced features of Datastore
to tweak the application

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.190
to get better
performance out it.

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:33.940
Again, the beautiful
thing about Datastore

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.980
is, you don't have to choose
anything ahead of time.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.120
You don't have to
say, I'm planning--

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.760
this application is
going to go viral

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:42.590
and I need a terabyte of data.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:45.250
You just say, I need a
place to store some data.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.330
You drop the data in.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:48.466
And you just keep putting
data in that application.

00:11:48.466 --> 00:11:49.840
And we will make
sure that we are

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.750
provisioned, very similar to
App Engine, to handle the load.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.830
So there's no
pre-thought on Datastore.

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.880
It's a really
wonderful thing to be

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:00.160
able to just get started without
making any decisions ahead

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:00.870
of time.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.500
And Datastore will work
with you to then scale up

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:07.080
your data to whatever the
size your application.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.870
Cloud Datastore, fully
managed NoSQL service.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.570
So it is, again-- I try
to position and talk

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.860
about it as a document
store type use case.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.330
There's no schema.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:17.074
You drop data in.

00:12:17.074 --> 00:12:18.740
We do some inference
on the data to make

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:22.430
it easy to store and make it
as performant as possible.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.190
But it is different
than Cloud SQL.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:27.590
So Cloud SQL is a dedicated
resource system where you say,

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:29.660
this is my VM,
this is my compute.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:32.790
And so you can do whatever
you want to that capacity.

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:34.950
With Datastore, it's
a multi-tenant system.

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.430
And so when you are
using the system,

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:39.550
we've put certain
limitations in place

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:42.380
to make sure that all of
your queries are fast.

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.850
So it's really designed to have
low latency, fast performance

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:48.297
all the time with,
again, no forethought.

00:12:48.297 --> 00:12:50.130
You don't have to worry
about schema design.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:51.860
You don't have to worry
about creating indexes.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:53.234
We're going to do
as much of that

00:12:53.234 --> 00:12:55.450
as we possibly can
automatically for you so

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.707
that the application will scale
once you put data into it.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:04.290
All right.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.623
Now we're starting to get a
little bit more interesting.

00:13:06.623 --> 00:13:09.390
So now we're starting
into more than a terabyte.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:11.590
So if you have more
than a terabyte of data,

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:14.350
you're generating some
interesting amounts of data.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:17.550
And this is where a lot of
the talks that we've had today

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:18.180
lean to.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.509
So when we talk about some
of the-- the Spotify use

00:13:20.509 --> 00:13:22.050
case and some of
the other ones, it's

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:23.810
all about how are
they aggregating

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:25.230
lots and lots of data?

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:27.210
If you're talking about
Internet of Things,

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.610
and IoT, and the
sensor data, where

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:32.960
do you take all of that sensor
data, which is essentially

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:36.230
a timestamp and an
identifier and a value?

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:38.310
Where do you drop all that
data in a single place

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.980
with super-high read
throughput so that you can then

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:44.610
do table scans on it and get
some value out of it later?

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.230
So this is where
Cloud Bigtable is

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.450
what I'm really excited
about for this use case.

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:51.033
So three different
ways that you could

00:13:51.033 --> 00:13:53.080
think about using
Cloud Bigtable.

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:54.990
So first of all, at
the device level.

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.200
And again, this could
be an IoT device.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.650
It could be a device running
Brillo or Weave or whatever

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:00.608
you happen to be using.

00:14:00.608 --> 00:14:03.595
And it's pushing telemetry
data back to the system.

00:14:03.595 --> 00:14:05.720
Now, the way that it pushes
data back to the system

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:07.650
is it pushes the
data to Pub/Sub.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.596
So every reading is a
single message on Pub/Sub.

00:14:10.596 --> 00:14:11.970
And what's great
about Pub/Sub is

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:14.250
it's this globally
available message

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.890
queue that allows you to push
data to it from anywhere.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.907
So that device
could literally be--

00:14:18.907 --> 00:14:20.990
it doesn't have to be
anywhere near a data center.

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:25.040
It could be an individual
mobile device, or IoT device.

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.140
It pushes the data
back to Pub/Sub

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:29.590
for asynchronous processing.

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.030
Once that message gets
into Pub/Sub, it's durable.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.945
It's not like running your own
message queue software on a VM.

00:14:34.945 --> 00:14:35.945
It's globally available.

00:14:35.945 --> 00:14:38.270
And again, Google Site
Reliability Engineers

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.700
are making sure that that's
always up and running.

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:41.820
The other thing
that's great about it,

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.569
and this is a recurring
theme, is that you

00:14:43.569 --> 00:14:45.280
don't have to provision it.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:48.120
You don't have to say,
I need 1,000 queries

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:49.590
per second of capacity.

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.520
You can just throw data at it.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:53.246
And as long as
your data-- as long

00:14:53.246 --> 00:14:55.120
as the number of requests
that you're sending

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:58.830
are lower than
millions of QPS, this

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.570
is something that we can handle
just because all of Google

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.339
runs on the same infrastructure.

00:15:03.339 --> 00:15:05.130
So when you're sending
messages to Pub/Sub,

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:06.630
you're using the
same infrastructure

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.470
that Google uses for all of
our asynchronous processing.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:11.760
And you can be confident
that the system's not

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:12.800
going to fall over.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.270
If you need to push
us a petabyte of data,

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.070
you can have very
high confidence

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:18.140
that we are going to
be able to handle that,

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:20.090
because we have lots of
experiencing handling

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:22.800
that much data, both for
our internal applications

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.630
as well as our other
customer applications.

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:27.370
So once those messages
get into Pub/Sub,

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:30.030
how do you process them and
get them into Cloud Bigtable?

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.820
One example, and again,
there's many ways

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:33.620
to do this, is Dataflow.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.310
So you can use Dataflow to
then pick up those messages,

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:39.170
do any transformations,
do any enriching, whatever

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:41.680
you want to do either in
a batch or a stream mode,

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.506
and push that data
into Cloud Bigtable.

00:15:44.506 --> 00:15:45.630
So that's one way to do it.

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:47.464
You could do this
also with Dataproc.

00:15:47.464 --> 00:15:49.130
If you have an Apache
Spark job that you

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:50.505
want to use for
streaming, if you

00:15:50.505 --> 00:15:53.070
have a MapReduce job that you
want to use in your Hadoop

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:54.890
cluster, or that you've
been using on prem

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.890
that you want to use in
the cloud, both of those

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.440
provide you the ability to then
take that data from Pub/Sub

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.210
and drop it into Cloud Bigtable.

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:03.780
Other ways that you can do this.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.681
So if you have a web device, or
if you have a web application

00:16:06.681 --> 00:16:08.930
speaking to a VMware where
you're hosting application,

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.320
that VM can speak directly
to Cloud Bigtable.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.700
So you can write directly
from x number of VMs

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:15.470
into Cloud Bigtable.

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:17.270
Another pattern that
we see very commonly

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:19.300
is microservice architecture.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:22.329
So if you have a
number of mobile apps,

00:16:22.329 --> 00:16:24.120
and those mobile apps
are all speaking back

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.870
to a microservice or
a RESTful endpoint,

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:30.680
you often need to collect
monitoring data from those.

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:34.180
This might be an event
user clicks a song

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:37.790
to play, in the Spotify use
case, or a user clicked a link,

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:39.470
or a user shut the
application or opened

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:42.270
the application, all of
that needs to be recorded.

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:45.346
So now, from the microservice,
you could push into Pub/Sub

00:16:45.346 --> 00:16:46.720
and then data flow
into Bigtable,

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:48.310
or you could push
directly into Bigtable.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:49.768
It all depends on
what your load is

00:16:49.768 --> 00:16:51.911
and what your architecture
wants to look like.

00:16:51.911 --> 00:16:53.410
What's great about
this is that once

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:55.770
you get the data
into Cloud Bigtable,

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:57.360
you have a lot of possibilities.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.460
So Cloud Bigtable is our
fully managed, NoSQL,

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:02.320
wide-column service.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.599
Cloud Bigtable, and
Bigtable internally,

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:09.210
and the Bigtable white paper,
spawned much of the Hadoop

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:10.730
ecosystem that we have today.

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:12.859
And so Cloud Bigtable
actually allows

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:16.510
you to use the original Cloud
Bigtable internal service

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:19.470
that much of Google
still runs on today.

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:22.310
We expose it via an HBase
client compatibility.

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:24.480
So we provide an
HBase client that you

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.470
can use to integrate with
your applications that

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.935
expect Hbase client.

00:17:29.935 --> 00:17:32.060
But what's really interesting
about Cloud Bigtable,

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:35.080
and this is something we spend
a lot of time talking about is,

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:40.125
while we expose it as an
HBase client, it is not HBase.

00:17:40.125 --> 00:17:41.500
And what's
interesting about that

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:45.530
is it's incredibly low latency,
random reads and writes.

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:47.600
And what this means is
that even though it's

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:50.440
meant to work in the
HBase analytic space,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.350
latencies are such that you can
actually serve live application

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:54.790
data out of this.

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:59.240
So you can actually, with like
six millisecond latencies in

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:02.014
and out, you can serve
live application data out

00:18:02.014 --> 00:18:04.680
of here, which is something that
most people wouldn't want to do

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.940
out of self-managed HBase.

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:11.000
Very interesting as
well, we're seeing

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.390
a lot of people that are
currently managing large HBase

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:15.517
and Cassandra clusters.

00:18:15.517 --> 00:18:17.100
This is an interesting
option for them

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.684
if they want a managed service.

00:18:18.684 --> 00:18:20.100
If you don't want
to roll your own

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:21.610
and manage your
own clusters, this

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:23.760
is a really interesting
option for people

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:26.360
to move those workloads
over and to have them

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:28.710
in a fully managed service
where Google SREs are

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:31.750
looking after your data.

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:35.080
The other thing that I'm
also very excited about

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.416
is a trend that we're
seeing externality,

00:18:37.416 --> 00:18:39.290
that we've seen inside
Google for many years,

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:42.940
is this concept of HTAP,
which is Hybrid Transactional

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:44.380
Analytical Processing.

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:47.220
And the idea here is that
you can keep your application

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:50.460
data in the same place that
you do your analytics queries.

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:53.700
And it doesn't require you to
ETL the data into multiple data

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:55.450
centers doing transformations.

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:57.970
You can leave all the
data in a single place

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.490
and run your
applications from it,

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.330
as well as run your
analytics queries.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:03.900
So obviously, this greatly
simplifies the architecture

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:04.880
of an application.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:06.546
And what's great about
this is that this

00:19:06.546 --> 00:19:08.950
is what we've been doing inside
of Google for many years.

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:12.930
Bigtable, BigQuery, things
that allow us to basically

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:15.890
have single solutions that
allow us to run our applications

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:17.640
as well as do our
analytics queries.

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:19.620
And Cloud Bigtable
is a perfect example

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:22.330
of how Google's internal
use case is starting

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:23.690
to manifest itself externally.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:29.090
Two other things to look at.

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:31.150
Very excited-- a
partner, Pythian,

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:34.640
recently introduced OpenTSDB
support for Cloud Bigtable.

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:41.380
So if you are looking for a very
well adopted API for doing time

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.760
series data, OpenTSDB
on top of Cloud Bigtable

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:48.830
is a really interesting solution

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:49.330
All right.

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.800
So the talk is only supposed
to go up to petabytes,

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.760
but we're Google, so we
have to talk about exabytes.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:59.445
So if you've gone
past petabytes of data

00:19:59.445 --> 00:20:02.780
and you're approaching
towards an exabyte of data,

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.204
how would you go
about doing that?

00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:07.370
So the first question is,
how would you actually get

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:08.540
that data into Google?

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.680
So we'll talk a little
bit about data ingestion

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:14.240
and how we're helping customers
get that data from either

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:19.010
on prem or other locations
onto Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:20.930
So the first one is
the Transfer Service.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:24.810
So we offer Google
Cloud Transfer Service.

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:28.140
And this is a tool for helping
to move around your data.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:31.680
So if you wanted to back
up all of your S3 data

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:34.700
into a GCS bucket, you could
do that, and set up a schedule

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.040
with Transfer Service.

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:41.330
If you want to move your data
from a GCS bucket, a Google

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:44.876
Cloud Storage bucket that's
currently in Standard,

00:20:44.876 --> 00:20:46.500
and after 30 days
you want to move data

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.070
into a GCS bucket
which is Nearline,

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:52.260
then you can do that
using Transfer Service.

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:55.110
So we provide that
as a tool to help

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:56.497
people move that data around.

00:20:56.497 --> 00:20:58.830
We're also trying to engage
really hard with our partner

00:20:58.830 --> 00:20:59.410
ecosystem.

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:01.880
We really want to
enable our partners

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:04.000
and help our partners
help our customers get

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:05.570
this data into the system.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.000
So the first one offline ingest.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:13.640
So we worked with Iron Mountain
in the US for offline ingest,

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:16.820
and Prime Focus
abroad for helping

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:18.060
people do offline ingest.

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.040
So if you have
petabytes of data,

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.580
or even lots of
terabytes of data

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.288
that you want to get
moved in the system,

00:21:23.288 --> 00:21:26.750
you can put them
into a hard drive

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:28.730
and you can ship those
to Iron Mountain.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:31.760
And they will upload that
data into Google Cloud Storage

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:32.750
for you.

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:36.260
Same thing outside
the United States.

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:38.210
The other area that
we've really invested

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:41.540
in since we announced
Nearline last year

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:45.150
was the filer in the
on-premise file systems.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:46.694
So in the middle
of last year, we

00:21:46.694 --> 00:21:48.110
announced the
ability of Nearline.

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:50.610
So within Google
Cloud Storage, we now

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.060
have three different
storage classes.

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:54.710
The first one is
Standard, which is

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:58.500
what people have been using to
host their static web content.

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:01.660
And when they're using their
data processing pipelines.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:03.840
Durable Reduced
Availability, which

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:06.710
in exchange for a slightly
lower SLA of availability,

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:09.170
you get a price break,
which is our second class.

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:10.670
And then the third
one we introduced

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:12.590
last year is Nearline.

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:17.060
And Nearline is our warm
storage archival solution

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:23.810
that allows you put your data
into a GCS bucket with Nearline

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:25.286
for basically archival.

00:22:25.286 --> 00:22:26.910
And as part of that,
we've been working

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:29.630
with all of our
partners in the space

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.810
to help facilitate
that for customers.

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:36.200
A lot of these partners have
huge enterprise footprints

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:39.360
on premise, and they want
to be able to back up

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:40.629
their files to the cloud.

00:22:40.629 --> 00:22:42.920
So how do we enable that and
make it easy for customers

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:43.610
to do that?

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.660
So working with the customers
like NetApp, Commvault, EMC,

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:49.310
Avere, making sure that
when you are running

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:51.570
those appliances on
prem, or some of them

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:54.580
in a Cloud Appliance, there
is the option for you to then

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:57.130
push that data into
Google Cloud Storage

00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:59.270
so that you can back
it up, and again,

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.750
have Google's SREs
looking after your data

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.120
to make sure that it
is safe and secure

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.792
and always there
when you need it.

00:23:09.515 --> 00:23:11.390
The last one that I
really like to talk about

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:12.170
is data pipelines.

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:15.010
And we've talked a little
bit about this earlier.

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:15.540
So great.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:19.380
I've got 500 petabytes of
data in Google Cloud Storage.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:20.320
Now what?

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:21.490
It's been sitting on prem.

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:23.270
I've never done
anything with it.

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:24.499
What is the value to me?

00:23:24.499 --> 00:23:26.290
And most people just
want it as optionality

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:27.240
in case of a disaster.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.740
They either need to keep it
from a compliance point of view,

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:31.380
or they just want to make sure
that if something happens,

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.000
they have a copy of their data.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.830
What's so great about this
integrated data platform

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:39.110
approach is that once you get
your data into the system,

00:23:39.110 --> 00:23:41.180
we can provide lots of
interesting tools for you

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:44.500
to be able to then take that
data and derive value from it

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:47.890
So first one we talked a little
bit about earlier is Dataflow.

00:23:47.890 --> 00:23:50.172
So if you want to either
do batch or streaming--

00:23:50.172 --> 00:23:52.130
and we've just recently
pushed this into Apache

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:54.880
as Apache Bean-- you
can use data flow

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:58.510
to run large factor stream
processes over your 500

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:02.740
petabytes of data to basically
clean it, extract value,

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:05.210
transform it, push
it into BigQuery,

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:06.690
do whatever you
need to do with it.

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:08.790
And again, fully
managed service.

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:11.700
If you need one petabyte of
data or 500 petabytes of data,

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:12.690
it's the same code.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.880
It's running in the
same infrastructure,

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:16.250
the same set of APIs.

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:18.970
So we can scalably
go through your data

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:21.220
and help you derive value.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:22.590
The second one is Dataproc.

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:25.360
And there's another talk just
after this one on Dataproc,

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:27.270
which I encourage
you guys to attend.

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.660
So Dataproc is really
helping customers

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:32.530
that are running
large on-prem Hadoop

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:34.460
installations to be
able to easily move

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:35.980
those workloads to the cloud.

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:37.950
So if you have a
MapReduce job that you've

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:41.335
been running on prem, once you
push all that data into Google

00:24:41.335 --> 00:24:43.710
Cloud Storage, you want to be
able to run that MapReduce.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:46.370
So Dataproc allows you to
just bring that MapReduce

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:49.080
into Google Cloud Platform,
and run that MapReduce

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:53.050
over your 500 petabytes of
data to get whatever value

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:57.350
or data extracted out
of that information.

00:24:57.350 --> 00:25:01.000
And then in many of these, if
you're doing that processing

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:02.780
and trying to extract
value, you often

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:05.040
need a place to put that data.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:07.740
So many times, people are
running these Dataflow jobs

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:10.830
and Dataproc jobs and
pushing data into BigQuery,

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:12.580
into a data warehouse,
where they can then

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:15.990
run their distribute queries.

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:18.377
Another thing that I'm very
excited about the direction

00:25:18.377 --> 00:25:20.210
we're going, which
really speaks to the idea

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:21.890
that you can put
your data anywhere

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.370
and we'll help you drive
value is within BigQuery,

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:26.430
you can actually do
federated queries.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:28.270
So you can do queries
against your data

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:29.550
that's already in BigQuery.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:32.600
But you can also do
joins and queries

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:34.260
against your data in GCS.

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:37.650
So if you have your data in an
Avrom or Parquet file in GCS,

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.450
you can run BigQuery
queries against that data

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:44.150
and potentially join it with
data that you have in BigQuery.

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:46.900
But where this becomes really
interesting is that we can then

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:49.574
expand those federated
queries out to Cloud Bigtable

00:25:49.574 --> 00:25:51.240
and Datastore and all
these other areas,

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:53.630
so that regardless of
where your data is,

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.630
you don't have to ETL it and
move it into another solution.

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:59.750
And you don't have to
ETL it into-- and by ETL,

00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:02.554
is Extract,
Transform, and Load--

00:26:02.554 --> 00:26:04.220
you don't have to
have a process to move

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:05.690
that data into BigQuery.

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:08.440
You can just do the
queries from BigQuery

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:10.386
and query your data in line.

00:26:10.386 --> 00:26:12.760
So that's where it starts to
get really compelling where,

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:16.300
wherever your data is in the
Google Cloud data platform,

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.700
you'll be able to then
access that data and drive

00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:21.490
value from it without having
to move it around and worry

00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:22.890
about where it is.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:29.880
One other thing, so in Dataproc,
so if you're using HBase,

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:31.210
we have HBase connectors.

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:34.100
So use that as an intermediate
point in Dataproc.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:35.759
Also, connectors for Dataflow.

00:26:35.759 --> 00:26:37.800
So making sure that all
of these various products

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:40.150
work really well
against Google Cloud

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.530
Storage to make it really easy
to move your files around.

00:26:45.250 --> 00:26:47.799
And then no talk about
the Google Cloud platform

00:26:47.799 --> 00:26:50.090
would be complete without
talking about our marketplace

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:51.550
solutions.

00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:53.280
So in our marketplace
solutions, we're

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:55.390
working with a great
bunch of partners

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:57.300
so that if one of
these managed solutions

00:26:57.300 --> 00:26:58.977
aren't the right
answer for you, then

00:26:58.977 --> 00:27:00.810
we're working with our
partners to make sure

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:02.686
that there is something
available to you that

00:27:02.686 --> 00:27:03.976
will hopefully meet your needs.

00:27:03.976 --> 00:27:05.230
So I've highlighted some here.

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:07.492
We've been working with
MongoDB so that they're

00:27:07.492 --> 00:27:08.700
available in the marketplace.

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:12.590
So if you need a MongoDB
cluster, you can do that.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:15.082
EnterpriseDB for
if you Postgres.

00:27:15.082 --> 00:27:17.040
Currently we don't offer
Postgres in Cloud SQL,

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.920
and so if you want to spin
up a Postgres cluster,

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.020
you can do so
through EnterpriseDB.

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:24.070
If you're looking to run
Cassandra, because maybe

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:27.200
your development team has a lot
of experience with Cassandra,

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:30.880
we offer a easy place
for you to take that data

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:33.290
and spin up a Cassandra,
either single node or cluster,

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:34.710
to be able to use that.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:35.870
And same thing with Redis.

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:38.170
So if you need Redis
for your application,

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:43.270
we provide the ability for you
to spin up a Google Compute

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:45.270
Engine VM with Redis so
you can then immediately

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:46.728
start using it in
your application.

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:53.990
So just to finish up,
data is everything.

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:55.750
It's at the core of
everything we do.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:57.861
We talk about a lot
of these applications,

00:27:57.861 --> 00:28:00.360
and there's amazing things you
can do with machine learning.

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:02.170
And as you start to
build your own models,

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:04.420
you're going to need a place
actually store your data.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:06.000
So where do you put
your training data?

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:07.666
It's going to be the
GCS bucket, or it's

00:28:07.666 --> 00:28:09.349
going to be a Bigtable table.

00:28:09.349 --> 00:28:11.890
And so there has to be a way to
get your data into the system

00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:14.390
so you can start to do all these
amazing things with machine

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:16.585
learning and other things
that we're talking about.

00:28:16.585 --> 00:28:17.960
And so what we're
doing is really

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:20.940
trying to make sure we have
that unified data platform

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:23.727
so that you can bring your data
into Google Cloud Platform,

00:28:23.727 --> 00:28:25.310
you can put it in
the application that

00:28:25.310 --> 00:28:27.800
makes sense for you so
that's cost effective,

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.300
it's the application or
framework or software

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:32.060
that you're accustomed
to working with.

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:34.143
And then once you do that,
we'll make it very easy

00:28:34.143 --> 00:28:38.890
for you to be able to move it,
transform it, and derive value

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:39.504
from it.

00:28:39.504 --> 00:28:41.170
And we want that to
all be in one place.

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:42.632
We don't want this
to be something

00:28:42.632 --> 00:28:43.840
that you have to think about.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:45.650
As I talked about
before, we're always

00:28:45.650 --> 00:28:47.390
trying to give you optionality.

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:49.640
We want you to be able to
choose the solution that you

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:50.700
think is best for you.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:53.540
And then as you scale up,
make it as easy as possible

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:55.080
to scale up your
application to meet

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.240
the needs of your
users and your business

00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:59.520
without having to make a
bunch of decisions up front.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.350
We don't want you
to have to provision

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:02.280
what you're doing in the future.

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:03.738
We don't want you
to have foresight

00:29:03.738 --> 00:29:06.644
into what your company
or business or use case

00:29:06.644 --> 00:29:07.560
is going to look like.

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:09.143
We want to make it
easy for you get up

00:29:09.143 --> 00:29:10.810
and running as
quickly as possible,

00:29:10.810 --> 00:29:14.110
and allow you to scale so your
business can be successful.

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:15.790
That's it.

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:17.420
Thank you very much.

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:20.470
[APPLAUSE]

